<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/3rd-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Cleomenean War</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">229–<a href="/years/222-bce">222 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Cleomenean War was fought from 229 to 222 BCE between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> under King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-iii">Cleomenes III</a> and the Achaean League. Cleomenes, who had carried out radical reforms at home, defeated the Achaeans repeatedly, prompting their leader Aratus to seek help from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-iii-doson">Antigonus III Doson</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>; the Macedonian and Achaean victory at Sellasia in 222 BCE ended Spartan power.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/cleomenean-war.jpg" alt="Cleomenean War" width="320" height="402" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MapMaster · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_Cleomenean_War-en.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cleomenes-iii">Cleomenes III</a> came to the Spartan throne in 236 or 235 BCE after deposing his father, Leonidas II, who had executed the reforming king <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/agis-iv">Agis IV</a>. In 229 BCE Cleomenes took Tegea, Mantineia, Caphyae and Orchomenus in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arcadia">Arcadia</a>, by treachery according to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>, at their own request according to other historians. When the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephor">ephors</a> then sent him to seize the Athenaeum near Belbina, a border point disputed with Megalopolis, the Achaean League declared war. Ptolemy III of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a> soon shifted his financial support from the Achaeans to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a>, judging it the more useful ally against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>Cleomenes routed the Achaean army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aratus-of-sicyon">Aratus of Sicyon</a> near Mount Lycaeum in 227 BCE and defeated another at Ladoceia, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lydiadas">Lydiadas</a> of Megalopolis was killed. He then had four of the five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephor">ephors</a> killed, divided the land into equal lots, made some <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perioeci">perioeci</a> citizens, trained 4,000 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoplite">hoplites</a> and armed them with the Macedonian sarissa. In 226 BCE he crushed the Achaeans at Dyme, and later took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argo">Argos</a> during the Nemean Games and won the surrender of Corinth. Aratus allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antigonus-iii-doson">Antigonus III Doson</a>, who demanded the citadel of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acrocorinth">Acrocorinth</a>.</p><p>Antigonus came south with 20,000 infantry and 1,300 cavalry. After a revolt at Argos Cleomenes abandoned his lines at the Isthmus, and in 223 BCE Antigonus took Tegea, Orchomenus, Mantinea and Heraea, confining him to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/laconia">Laconia</a>. When Ptolemy withdrew his money, Cleomenes freed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/helots">helots</a> who could pay five minae, and he surprised and burned Megalopolis. In the summer of 222 BCE Antigonus, with 29,200 men by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/polybius">Polybius</a>'s count, attacked the Spartans holding the hills of Evas and Olympus at Sellasia. Eucleidas, the king's brother, was killed, and of 6,000 Spartans who fought only 200 survived.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Cleomenes fled by ship from Gythium to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/egypt">Egypt</a>. Antigonus entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> as its first foreign conqueror, treated the people generously, revoked the reforms and restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ephor">ephors</a>, but did not restore the kings, and within three days left to deal with a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dardanians">Dardani</a> invasion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-macedon">Macedon</a>. Sparta's power collapsed and it fell to a series of tyrants. In Alexandria Ptolemy III promised Cleomenes an army, but died; under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ptolemy-iv-philopator">Ptolemy IV</a> he was put under house arrest, and in 219 BCE, after a failed attempt to raise the city, he and his friends killed themselves.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Cleomenean War” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
